LINK(2)                        P32W Unix                         LINK(2)

NAME
       link - make a hard link to a file

SYNOPSIS
       #include <unistd.h>

       int link(const char *old, const char *new);

DESCRIPTION
       link makes the new directory entry new for the existing
       file old and raises its link count. The two names are then
       equal: neither is the original. Both must be on the same
       filesystem, and the directory that will hold new must be
       writable. Only the super-user may link a directory.

RETURN VALUE
       0 on success; -1 on an error, with errno set.

ERRORS
       ENOENT  old does not exist, or a directory in either path
               does not exist.
       ENOTDIR A component of a path prefix is not a directory.
       EACCES  Search permission is denied on a directory in a
               path, or the directory for new is not writable.
       EEXIST  new exists.
       EPERM   old is a directory and the caller is not the
               super-user.
       EXDEV   old and new are on different filesystems.
       ENOSPC  The directory for new cannot grow.
       EFAULT  A path is a bad address.
       ENAMETOOLONG
               A path is 256 characters or more, or a component
               is longer than 28.

SEE ALSO
       unlink(2), rename(2), ln(1), stat(2)

BUGS
       There are no symbolic links. The link count is not checked
       against a limit (EMLINK is never returned).
